critic.difference_if_any:
The reduction leaves only engineering on top of the 1-D lattice learner:
- a multiclass coset-to-label table with a collision check, instead of a single positive class;
- FIFO eviction with anchor migration;
- a batched consolidation schedule (clock B / COMMIT);
- an exact episodic cache that answers before compilation.

None of these changes the hypothesis class, the update or the inference rule. The learning rule is the standard closure (gcd/HNF) of positive examples, so it is not a new learning rule. Relative to the neural families on the list (RNN, SSM, linear attention, Hopfield), the card does not reduce in any meaningful sense. A Turing-style encoding as a recurrent machine is trivial and not informative.

toy_example.description:
Part 1 uses the 3-concept automaton T = [[0,.5,.5],[1,0,0],[0,1,0]] with pi0 = one-hot on concept 0, V = 3 and eps = 0.05, on the context a c b a (tokens 0, 2, 1, 0). It prints three lag-one credits, rounded to 3 decimals:
- c's credit with a already bound;
- the counterfactual credit with a unbound;
- b's credit.
It also prints the argmax bindings and the next-token distribution, rounded to 4 decimals. The pair (a, b) never occurs in the context, yet b gets 0.4342. The program's loop is 0-indexed, but its credits are those of u4 to u6.

Part 2 uses the symmetric diamond (K = V = 4, eps = 0.05) on the walk a b d a c d a b d. It checks that rows 1 and 2 of C agree within 1e-12, and that the predicted probability of d at the third post-branch position is 2.05/2.2.

falsifiers:
In the inferred-binding protocol (2400 queries), core accuracy on unshown cues is below 0.9. The separation claims are also refuted if any baseline exceeds its stated bound on the same episodes and codes:
- in-context least squares H^T (G + lambda I)^{-1} q exceeds 0.3 for any lambda in {1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1, 1, 10}, in this protocol or in the overcomplete-recall protocol;
- hard top-2 retrieval exceeds 0.1;
- softmax kernel memory exceeds 0.2 for any beta in {0.25, ..., 64}.

known_relatives.claimed_difference:
The novelty is not OMP itself. It is the closed loop:
- the dictionary is a set of one-shot episodes;
- the same pursuit decides what is written (u4);
- the pursuit coefficients transfer from keys to values to predict tokens.
Online dictionary learning instead updates atoms by gradient and stores no value channel.

state.meaning:
The stream has n steps z_1..z_n. Each step is DEMO(X_t, y_t) or QUERY(X_t). X_t is a nonempty set of at most w cue tokens, drawn from a cue vocabulary of U tokens. y_t is one of V output tokens.

Fixed constants:
- s: pursuit sparsity (s = 2 in all claims);
- d: store capacity;
- eps > 0: explanation tolerance (1e-6 in claims);
- tau > 0: selection floor (1e-9).

On a DEMO step the model first emits its prediction p_t for y_t, and then the label is revealed.

